Given, $f(x)=x^4-x-10$
We assume $x_0=2$ is the approximate root of $f(x)$.
Then, $h=-\frac{f\left(x_0\right)}{f\left(x_0\right)}=-\frac{f(2)}{f(2)}\begin{array}{ll}\Rightarrow & h=-\left[\frac{(2)^4-2-10}{4(2)^3-1}\right] \\ \Rightarrow & h=-\left[\frac{16-12}{31}\right]=\frac{-4}{31} \\ \Rightarrow & h=-0.129\end{array}
$\therefore$ Positive square root of $f(x)$ by Newton-Raphson method,
$\begin{aligned} x_1=x_0+h & =2+(-0.129) \\ & =2-0.129=1.871\end{aligned}

Given function is
$f(x)=2 e^x-a e^{-x}+(2 a+1) x-3$
For increasing function, $\quad f(x)\gt0, \forall x\begin{array}{lr}\Rightarrow & f^{\prime}(x)=2 e^x+a e^{-x}+(2 a+1)\gt0 \\ \Rightarrow & \left(2 e^x+1\right)+a\left(e^{-x}+2\right)\gt0 \\ \Rightarrow & a\left(e^{-x}+2\right)\gt-\left(1+2 e^x\right) \\ \Rightarrow & a\gt\frac{-\left(1+2 e^x\right)}{\left(e^{-x}+2\right)}\end{array}
$\begin{array}{lrl}\Rightarrow & a & \gt\frac{-\left(1+2 e^x\right)}{\left(1+2 e^x\right)} \cdot e^x \\ \Rightarrow & a & \gt-e^x \\ \because & e^x \in(0, \infty) & \Rightarrow-e^x \in(-\infty, 0)\end{array}
and $a\gt-e^x\begin{array}{ll}\therefore & a \in[0, \infty)\end{array}$

Given, for $x, y \in Nf(x+y)=f(x) \cdot f(y)$
Then, function will be of the form
$f(x)=a^x$, where $a \in N \quad[\because a \neq 1]\begin{array}{ll}\because & f(1)=3 \\ \Rightarrow & f(1)=a^1=3 \\ \Rightarrow & a=3\end{array}
$\therefore$ Function is $f(x)=3^x$.
Now, $\sum_{x=1}^n f(x)=120\Rightarrow \quad \sum_{x=1}^n 3^x=120\Rightarrow 3+3^2+3^3+\ldots+3^n=120\Rightarrow \quad \frac{3\left(3^{n}-1\right)}{3-1}=120\begin{array}{ll}\Rightarrow & 3^n=1+\frac{120 \times 2}{3} \\ \Rightarrow & 3^n=81=3^4\end{array}
So, $n=4$

It is given, $\cot A, \cot B$ and $\cot C$ are in AP .
$\begin{array}{ll}\Rightarrow & 2 \cot B=\cot A+\cot C \\ \Rightarrow & 2 \frac{\cos B}{\sin B}=\frac{\cos A}{\sin A}+\frac{\cos C}{\sin C} \\ \because & \frac{\sin A}{a}=\frac{\sin B}{b}=\frac{\sin C}{c}=k \\ \Rightarrow & \frac{2 \cos B}{k b}=\frac{\cos A}{a k}+\frac{\cos C}{c k} \\ \Rightarrow & \frac{2 \cos B}{b}=\frac{\cos A}{a}+\frac{\cos C}{c}\end{array}
$\begin{aligned} & \Rightarrow \quad \frac{2}{b}\left[\frac{a^2+c^2-b^2}{2 a c}\right]=\frac{1}{a}\left[\frac{b^2+c^2-a^2}{2 b c}\right] \\ &+\frac{1}{c}\left[\frac{a^2+b^2-c^2}{2 a b}\right]\end{aligned}\begin{aligned} & \Rightarrow \quad \frac{2\left(a^2+c^2-b^2\right)}{2 a b c}=\frac{1}{2 a b c} \\ & {\left[\left(b^2+c^2-a^2\right)+\left(a^2+b^2-c^2\right)\right] }\end{aligned}\begin{array}{rlrl}\Rightarrow & & 2\left(a^2+c^2-b^2\right) & =2 b^2 \\ \Rightarrow & a^2+c^2 & =2 b^2\end{array}
$\therefore a^2, b^2$ and $c^2$ are in AP.

